# Spring Security 6 Migration Guide

## Overview
Spring Security 6 removes deprecated WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter and requires component-based configuration.

## Key Changes

### 1. WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter is Removed
In Spring Security 5.x and earlier, configuration was done by extending `WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter`:

```java
// OLD - No longer works in Spring Security 6
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter {

    @Override
    protected void configure(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        // configuration
    }
}
```

### 2. New Component-Based Configuration
In Spring Security 6, use bean definitions instead:

```java
// NEW - Spring Security 6 approach
@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {

    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .csrf(csrf -> csrf.disable())
            .sessionManagement(session -> session
                .sessionCreationPolicy(SessionCreationPolicy.STATELESS))
            .exceptionHandling(exception -> exception
                .authenticationEntryPoint((request, response, ex) -> {
                    response.sendError(HttpServletResponse.SC_UNAUTHORIZED, ex.getMessage());
                }))
            .authorizeHttpRequests(authz -> authz
                .requestMatchers(HttpMethod.POST, "/api/users").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers("/api/auth/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers("/h2-console/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers("/actuator/health").permitAll()
                .anyRequest().authenticated());

        http.headers(headers -> headers
            .frameOptions(frame -> frame.disable())); // For H2 console

        return http.build();
    }

    @Bean
    public UserDetailsService userDetailsService() {
        // return your UserDetailsService implementation
    }

    @Bean
    public AuthenticationManager authenticationManager(AuthenticationConfiguration config)
            throws Exception {
        return config.getAuthenticationManager();
    }

    @Bean
    public PasswordEncoder passwordEncoder() {
        return new BCryptPasswordEncoder();
    }
}
```

### 3. Annotation Changes

#### Method Security
- `@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity` is deprecated
- Use `@EnableMethodSecurity` instead (with same parameters)

```java
// OLD
@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)

// NEW
@EnableMethodSecurity(prePostEnabled = true)
```

### 4. Lambda DSL
Spring Security 6 uses lambda DSL for configuration (recommended):
- More concise
- Better IDE support
- Type-safe

```java
// Example using lambda DSL
.authorizeHttpRequests(authz -> authz
    .requestMatchers("/public/**").permitAll()
    .requestMatchers("/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
    .anyRequest().authenticated())
```

### 5. HttpServletResponse Import Change
```java
// OLD
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;

// NEW
import jakarta.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;
```

## Migration Checklist
- [ ] Remove `extends WebSecurityConfigurerAdapter`
- [ ] Convert `configure(HttpSecurity http)` to `SecurityFilterChain` bean
- [ ] Convert `configure(AuthenticationManagerBuilder auth)` to `UserDetailsService` bean
- [ ] Update `@EnableGlobalMethodSecurity` to `@EnableMethodSecurity`
- [ ] Replace all `javax.servlet.*` imports with `jakarta.servlet.*`
- [ ] Convert to lambda DSL for cleaner code
- [ ] Test all security configurations

## References
- [Spring Security 6 Documentation](https://spring.io/blog/2023/06/21/spring-security-6-0-0-released)
- [Spring Security Migration Guide](https://spring.io/blog/2022/02/21/spring-security-without-the-websecurityconfigureradapter)

