# RestTemplate to RestClient Migration Guide

## Overview
Spring Framework 6.1+ introduces RestClient as a modern replacement for RestTemplate. RestClient is more intuitive, immutable by default, and aligns with Spring Boot 3.2.

## Key Differences

### RestTemplate (Legacy)
- Mutable and stateful
- Uses method chaining with optional parameters
- Synchronous only
- Less intuitive API for complex scenarios

### RestClient (Modern)
- Immutable and stateless
- Lambda-based fluent API
- Better exception handling
- More intuitive for building complex requests

## Migration Pattern

### 1. Basic GET Request

```java
// OLD - RestTemplate
ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, Map.class);

// NEW - RestClient
Map response = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .body(Map.class);
```

### 2. POST Request with Headers

```java
// OLD - RestTemplate
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setContentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON);
HttpEntity<Map<String, String>> request = new HttpEntity<>(payload, headers);
ResponseEntity<Void> response = restTemplate.postForEntity(url, request, Void.class);

// NEW - RestClient
restClient.post()
    .uri(url)
    .contentType(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
    .body(payload)
    .retrieve()
    .toBodilessEntity();
```

### 3. GET with Headers and Response Handling

```java
// OLD - RestTemplate
HttpHeaders headers = new HttpHeaders();
headers.setAccept(Collections.singletonList(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON));
HttpEntity<?> request = new HttpEntity<>(headers);
ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.exchange(url, HttpMethod.GET, request, Map.class);

// NEW - RestClient
Map response = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .accept(MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON)
    .retrieve()
    .body(Map.class);
```

### 4. DELETE Request

```java
// OLD - RestTemplate
restTemplate.delete(url);

// NEW - RestClient
restClient.delete()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .toBodilessEntity();
```

## Creating RestClient Bean

```java
@Configuration
public class RestClientConfig {

    @Bean
    public RestClient restClient(RestClient.Builder builder) {
        return builder
            .baseUrl("https://api.example.com")
            .build();
    }
}
```

Or for simple cases, inject `RestClient.Builder`:

```java
@Service
public class ExternalApiService {

    private final RestClient restClient;

    public ExternalApiService(RestClient.Builder builder) {
        this.restClient = builder.baseUrl("https://api.example.com").build();
    }
}
```

## Error Handling

### RestTemplate Error Handling
```java
try {
    ResponseEntity<Map> response = restTemplate.getForEntity(url, Map.class);
} catch (RestClientException e) {
    // handle error
}
```

### RestClient Error Handling
```java
Map response = restClient.get()
    .uri(url)
    .retrieve()
    .onStatus(status -> !status.is2xxSuccessful(),
        (request, response) -> {
            throw new CustomException("API Error: " + response.getStatusCode());
        })
    .body(Map.class);
```

## Migration Checklist
- [ ] Add RestClient dependency (included in Spring Boot 3.2)
- [ ] Create RestClient bean instead of RestTemplate
- [ ] Convert all `getForEntity()` calls to lambda DSL
- [ ] Convert all `postForEntity()` calls
- [ ] Convert all `exchange()` calls
- [ ] Convert all `delete()` calls
- [ ] Update error handling
- [ ] Remove RestTemplate bean definition

## Dependencies
RestClient is available in Spring Framework 6.1+ (included in Spring Boot 3.2 by default).

## References
- [Spring RestClient Documentation](https://docs.spring.io/spring-framework/reference/integration/rest-client.html)
- [Spring Boot 3.2 Release Notes](https://spring.io/blog/2023/11/30/spring-boot-3-2-0-released)

